  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nWe are to find an upper bound for the Erds minimum overlap problem constant C, specifically improving upon the current record of 0.3808. The current program uses a multi-resolution approach with Thue-Morse as a base and then optimizes it at 128, 256, and 512 points. The key limitation is that it relies on: (1) gradient-based local search that may get stuck in plateaus, (2) a fixed set of hyperparameters, (3) optimization only along the axes set by the parent function, and (4) limited exploration at higher resolutions. Our improvement should address these by: (a) using multi-gradient optimization to explore low-dimensional changes, (b) implementing data-driven hyperparameter scheduling, (c) upgrading the neighbor exploration to consider damping and interpolation between parents, (d) explicitly targeting scores below 0.3808, and (e) incorporating adaptive data analysis.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n- Representation: h(x) as a step function sampled at n_point intervals over [0,2]\n- Constraints: 0 <= h[i] <= 1 for all i, sum(h) = n_points/2\n- Objective: C\u2085 = max_k \u222b h(x)[1-h(x+k)]dx = (2/n)*max_k correlation(h, [1-h])[k]\n- Optimization: Minimize C\u2085 subject to constraints\n\nWe use a two-stage approach:\n(a) Multi-start gradient descent with momentum and adaptive learning rate\n(b) Local search only around feasible points satisfying both 0<=h<=1 and sum=1.\nAssume dimensions are powers of two (64, 128, 256, 512).\n\n3. Implementation procedure\nStep-by-step:\nA. Initialization:\n- Get parent solution from initial_h_values if available (n=64)\n- Define hyperparameters: max_n=512, max_seconds=1100, time_scale=100 (seconds per doubling)\n- Keep track of: best_h, best_score, history\n\nB. Outer loop (cascading optimization):\nFor n in [64, 128, 256, 512]:\n  - If n=64: start from parent (if available) and random balanced vector\n  - Else: upsample best_h from previous tier and generate random balanced vector\n  - For each candidate: run adaptive gradient descent with damping\n\nC. Adaptive gradient descent (for each resolution):\n- Initial stepsize = 0.5 if n<=128 else 0.3\n- Damping factor = 0.95\n- Anneal per 500 steps: stepsize *= 0.98**(iteration/500)\n- Use projected parameters to keep within [0,1]\n- After warmup, switch to SLSQP for precision\n- Stop if score converges or time runs out\n\nD. Scoring and gradient calculation:\n- Compute_gradient(h) = -sum(delta*h) factor proportional to lag overlap\n- Allow only local changes that can be projected\n\nE. Advanced explorations:\n- Add perturbations: uniform, Gaussian, and binary flips\n- Maintain parent solutions across resolutions via upsampling\n- Use line search over damping parameters (alpha in [0.5, 0.99])\n\nF. Termination handling:\n- Switch to adaptive damping schedule when gradient flattens\n- Fallback to parent if solution unsatisfactory at any resolution\n- Return best solution found\n\n4. Search choices and variations\n- Introduce three hyperparameter profiles for different resolutions:\n  Profile 1 (n=64-128): high learning rate, fewer SLSQP iterations\n  Profile 2 (n=128-256): learning rate decay, increased damping\n  Profile 3 (n=256-512): auto-damping schedule, adaptive neighborhood\n- Implement multiple perturbation mechanisms: \n  (1) Binary flip with annealed probability\n  (2) Gaussian noise addition\n  (3) Uniform interpolation between parents\n\n5. Numerical robustness\n- Always verify score against the original evaluator\n- Use helper functions to enforce:\n  (a) Feasibility via projection\n  (b) Gradient bounds via saturation\n  (c) Sum enforcement via shifting\n- Fallback strategy: use least solution if optimization fails\n\n6. Runtime safeguards\n- Time tracking: interval=0.05 seconds by default\n- Memory: arrays with length n; n<=512 handles all\n- Termination: max iterations governed by time, not values\n- Fallback: if SLSQP fails, use Thue-Morse from higher resolution\n- Score comparison: always use original correlation evaluator",
